{"ts": "2026-07-06T03:53:45.513953+00:00", "run_id": "20260706T035323Z_715c2831", "event": "model_response", "turn": 6, "content": "FINAL ANSWER:\n\n### Executive Summary\nThe experiment `6d6e37e2` evaluated the analytical aggregation performance of DuckDB across three data scales: **Small (100,000 rows)**, **Medium (1,000,000 rows)**, and **Large (10,000,000 rows)**. \n\nThe results indicate that DuckDB scales **highly sub-linearly** (i.e., performance scales much better than linear). An increase of 100x in data volume (from 100K to 10M rows) resulted in only an **11.85x** increase in query execution time. This efficiency is typical of DuckDB’s vectorized execution engine, which amortizes fixed query planning and engine setup overhead as the data volume grows.\n\n---\n\n### Performance & Scaling Metrics\n\nBelow is a summary of the performance across each partition scale. Each scale was run with a replication factor of 3.\n\n| Scale Label | Rows | Mean Duration (seconds) | Mean Duration (ms) | Std Dev (ms) | Coeff. of Variation (%) | Provenance Fragment Key |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Small** | 100,000 | `0.006932` | 6.93 ms | 0.15 ms | 2.21% | `e_6d6e37e2__duckdb_benchmark_analytical_wall__small` |\n| **Medium** | 1,000,000 | `0.013993` | 13.99 ms | 1.43 ms | 10.24% | `e_6d6e37e2__duckdb_benchmark_analytical_wall__medium` |\n| **Large** | 10,000,000 | `0.082171` | 82.17 ms | 1.75 ms | 2.13% | `e_6d6e37e2__duckdb_benchmark_analytical_wall__large` |\n\n---\n\n### Scaling Analysis\n\nTo understand how DuckDB performance scales, we compare the relative increase in data rows against the relative increase in execution time:\n\n1. **Small $\\rightarrow$ Medium ($10\\times$ Data Increase)**:\n   * **Data Scale:** $100,000 \\rightarrow 1,000,000$ rows ($10\\times$)\n   * **Execution Time:** $6.93\\text{ ms} \\rightarrow 13.99\\text{ ms}$ ($2.02\\times$ increase)\n   * **Behavior:** Highly sub-linear. This indicates that at $100,000$ rows, query execution is heavily dominated by static overhead (e.g., query parsing, physical planning, schema lookup, thread pool scheduling). \n\n2. **Medium $\\rightarrow$ Large ($10\\times$ Data Increase)**:\n   * **Data Scale:** $1,000,000 \\rightarrow 10,000,000$ rows ($10\\times$)\n   * **Execution Time:** $13.99\\text{ ms} \\rightarrow 82.17\\text{ ms}$ ($5.87\\times$ increase)\n   * **Behavior:** Sub-linear. While the execution cost begins to scale more in step with the actual number of scanned tuples, it is still significantly better than linear.\n\n3. **Small $\\rightarrow$ Large ($100\\times$ Data Increase)**:\n   * **Data Scale:** $100,000 \\rightarrow 10,000,000$ rows ($100\\times$)\n   * **Execution Time:** $6.93\\text{ ms} \\rightarrow 82.17\\text{ ms}$ ($11.85\\times$ increase)\n   * **Behavior:** Overall, DuckDB demonstrates exceptional amortization of costs. Vectorized processing (processing columnar chunks of ~2048 records using cache-friendly instructions) combined with parallelization allows the engine to handle millions of extra tuples with minimal incremental overhead.\n\n---\n\n### Replication and Stability Notes\n* The measurements are highly stable, as evidenced by low Coefficients of Variation (CV) for the **Small** ($2.21\\%$) and **Large** ($2.13\\%$) runs. \n* The **Medium** scale demonstrated slightly higher variance ($10.24\\%$ CV, with individual run times ranging from $12.71\\text{ ms}$ to $15.54\\text{ ms}$), likely due to standard OS thread scheduling variability or memory allocation pauses during the slightly larger query footprint, but remains well within acceptable bounds.", "content_len": 3424, "tool_calls": [], "empty_content": false, "recovered_call_reason": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 2726, "prompt_tokens": 4414, "total_tokens": 7140, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 1642, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": 1084, "image_tokens": null,